{"word": "swallow", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "swallow", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈswɒl.əʊ/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈswɒl.ə/", "tags": ["Northumbria"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈswɑ.loʊ/", "tags": ["General American", "dialectal"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈswɑ.loʊ̯]", "tags": ["General American", "dialectal"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈswɑlə/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "swallows", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "swallowing", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "swallowed", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "swallowed", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "swalow", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}, {"word": "swolow", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To cause (food, drink etc.) to pass from the mouth into the stomach; to take into the stomach through the throat.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "What the liquor was I do not know, but it was not so strong but that I could swallow it in great gulps and found it less burning than my burning throat.", "reference": "[1898], J[ohn] Meade Falkner, Moonfleet, London; Toronto, Ont.: Jonathan Cape, published 1934, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Clothes are to be worn and food is to be swallowed: they remain trapped in the physical world.", "reference": "2011 April 21, Jonathan Jones, The Guardian:"}, {"text": "The four-and-one-half-day trial was centered on acts that neither she nor prosecutors dispute: On July 13, 2012, she drove her Lexus S.U.V. erratically after swallowing Zolpidem, a generic form of the sleep medication Ambien.", "reference": "2014 February 28, Joseph Berger, “Fast Acquittal for Kennedy, Whose Name Put Prosecutors in Bind”, in The New York Times, →ISSN, archived from the original on 28 Aug 2022:"}], "synonyms": ["consume", "devour", "eat", "gulp"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To take (something) in so that it disappears; to consume, absorb.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The necessary provision of the life swallows the greatest part of their time.", "reference": "a. 1705, John Locke, “Of the Conduct of the Understanding”, in Posthumous Works of Mr. John Locke: […], London: […] A[wnsham] and J[ohn] Churchill, […], published 1706, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "His body, like so many others swallowed by the ocean's hungry maw, was never found.", "reference": "2010 October 28, “What are the wild waves saying”, in The Economist:"}, {"text": "Elsewhere still, they'd managed to find the wreck of a Japanese midget submarine, and so, when the battleships were done being swallowed by the seabed, some efforts were being made to haul this up as well.", "reference": "2020 November 11, Drachinifel, 25:13 from the start, in The Salvage of Pearl Harbor Pt 1 - The Smoke Clears, archived from the original on 22 Oct 2022:"}], "synonyms": ["absorb", "engulf", "incorporate", "swallow up", "absorb", "add on", "assimilate", "co-opt", "corporate", "incorporate", "incorpse", "integrate", "subsume", "swallow"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To take food down into the stomach; to make the muscular contractions of the oesophagus to achieve this, often taken as a sign of nervousness or strong emotion.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["My throat was so sore that I was unable to swallow."], "quotes": [{"text": "She swallowed nervously then, appearing near sick with what she had to say.", "reference": "1979, VC Andrews, Flowers in the Attic:"}], "synonyms": ["gulp"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To accept easily or without questions; to believe, accept.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "this humbug was readily swallowed by men who were supposed to be intelligent,", "reference": "1920, Romain Rolland, translated by Katherine Miller, Clerambault:"}, {"text": "Most newspapers we saw swallowed whole an S.R. estimate that it would cost £20m to equip the Region with point heaters.", "reference": "1962 March, “Lessons of the Freeze-up”, in Modern Railways, page 146:"}, {"text": "Be careful of the Commie Lies / swallow them and freedom dies / The USA must realize / that she's the biggest prize.", "reference": "1965, “Commie Lies”, in Fascist Threat / Commie Lies, performed by Janet Greene:"}, {"text": "Americans swallowed his tale because they wanted to.", "reference": "2011 April 22, Madeleine Bunting, The Guardian:"}], "synonyms": ["buy", "credit"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To engross; to appropriate; usually with up.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Homer excels […] in this, that he swallowed up the honour of those who succeeded him.", "reference": "1715–1720, Homer, translated by Alexander Pope, “Book preface”, in The Iliad of Homer, volume (please specify |volume=I to VI), London: […] W[illiam] Bowyer, for Bernard Lintott […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": ["absorb", "monopolize", "occupy", "take over"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To retract; to recant.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["to swallow one's opinions"], "quotes": [{"text": "Left her in her tears, and dried not one of them with his comfort; swallowed his vows whole", "reference": "c. 1603–1604 (date written), William Shakespeare, “Measure for Measure”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene i]:"}], "synonyms": ["disavow", "take back", "abjure", "backpedal", "disavow", "disown", "eat one's words", "recall", "recant", "retract", "revo